<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Fabricator.Bridge.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Runs on both TFMs (inherited): net8.0 is the deployment floor, net10.0 the default. -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ⚠ THERE IS NO ProjectReference TO Fabricator.Bridge, AND ADDING ONE WOULD BREAK THIS TIER.

    Fabricator.Bridge project-references engineered-wood (a git SUBMODULE), plus Apache.Arrow, the
    Fabric SDK, SqlClient, the AWS and Azure SDKs. Referencing it here would make these tests require an
    initialised submodule and a large restore — and tier 0's whole point is that it runs with no C++, no
    vcpkg and no submodules, in about two minutes.

    So this project COMPILES SELECTED BRIDGE SOURCE FILES directly. The admission rule is the
    constraint that keeps the tier cheap:

        a Bridge file belongs here only if its closure is the .NET base class library.

    That is a feature, not a workaround. It rewards keeping decision logic — parsing, resolution,
    rendering — free of the Arrow/SDK boundary, which is exactly what makes it testable. A file that
    cannot be admitted is telling you it has business logic entangled with I/O.

    To test something new: if it is already BCL-only, link it below. If it is not, extract the
    decidable part into a file that is (FabricVariableLibraryFormat.cs was split out of
    FabricVariableFunctions.cs for precisely this reason), and link that.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\Fabricator.Bridge\FabricApi\FabricVariableLibraryFormat.cs"
             Link="Linked\FabricVariableLibraryFormat.cs" />
    <!--
      The Fabric SQL endpoint host/connstr derivation. Admissible because its closure is the BCL (it hand-rolls
      both the base32 decode — the BCL has none — and the connection-string parse, rather than reaching for
      SqlConnectionStringBuilder, precisely so it can be tested here). It is also the code that most NEEDS
      offline tests: it decodes an UNDOCUMENTED host encoding observed on one tenant, and its required
      behaviour on anything unexpected is to return null rather than a plausible-looking wrong workspace id.
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\Fabricator.Bridge\FabricApi\FabricSqlEndpointHost.cs"
             Link="Linked\FabricSqlEndpointHost.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
